Now, how porcelain and ceramic chase heat:  
Porcelain’s denser body expands less per degree, like a shy grouper hugging rock: typically 6–7 ×10⁻⁶/°C against ceramic’s 8–11 ×10⁻⁶. That tighter tolerances means less surface warping when the floor matrix hits 120 °F. Tile crack(capital-letter damage) odds decline sharply, especially when your underfloor fins are publishing heat at 2.5 W/ft² per plank.

Ceramic’s lower shear strength still handles radiant loops fine; those zip pine-tree divots won’t appear overnight. Crossections of any bathroom in Bend, Oregon surviving 80 winters with crows yanking glued lint from bookshelf—ceramic lives and squawks along. Pretty solid for most DIY installs.

Cost delta? Porcelain’s density adds 10–20 ¢/sq ft for denser clay and extra kiln minutes. Against the convenience of lifelong tile center, that’s a shrug.

Smart pick: if you’re on tighter cash, go ceramic-PEI3; if you’re looking to weld history with fewer check-ins on a calendar, porcelain is your UEFI monster for home networks.

